vious generation had to  be to do with assimilation the notion  that in the 60s there was you know this  notion of what the American white  American dream was that we should all  integrate ourselves in well the model  that I'm presenting here is radically  different I'm saying it's not about all  of us becoming white bread it's about  how to D Sigma ties the groups that are  stigmatized and understand how this  happened as a social process so in my  mind this is exactly the kind of work  that see far as enabled and I think of  knowledge production as a process if  slow process of on the one hand  production and on the other hand  diffusion and what i'm doing here is  diffusion but all of us do diffusion all  the time I had this week a blog in the  Harvard Business Review come out on this  paper on the Trump and the white  working-class and you know who reads the  Harvard Business Review so all of us are  putting our stuff out there little by  little and knowledge trickles out and we  cannot control how it trickles out but I  think it's a process of social change  that is slow but I also think that is  the kind of society  that the CFR wants to stand for and I  would say as a proud Canadian /  québécois that it's also what Canadian  society wants to stand for at a time  when it's important for Canada to give  lessons to the u.s. I think we're in a  good position to do this right now thank  you  [Applause]  Michelle thank you very much for that  really really wonderful  address we're a little over time but I  think we have time for one or two short  questions and short answers Tim the  us-brazil in Israel  why not Canada well the reason we chose  those three cases I alluded to this very  quickly is really has to do with the way  that social boundaries are structured in  those three societies we had in mind a  society that is racially diverse with  very rigid boundaries very flexible  boundaries Brazil and the you know the  walls and the segregation in Israel is  extremely strong and can in the u.s.  thought we thought was somewhat in the  middle we had a conference prior to the  project where we invited some Canadians  from French some Irish people there were  seven cases and when you put together a  big collaborative project like this a  lot of it has to do with who really  wants to be involved so it didn't work  out with the Canadians but Michelle  again I want to thank you for just a  wonderful oh you have a question then  I'll let Lee in a CFR fellow from the  University of Guelph asked a question  the end okay thank you so I'm wondering  if you could say a little bit more about  gender so some of your cases it seemed  like gender issues were very salient if  I think about membership I think that  women are clearly seen as members of  like some of them some of the societies  that you've been talking about if I  think about moral worth often people  think that women are more morally worthy  than men but if I think about issues  around competence and neoliberalism and  being the most productive person you can  be then we start to see gender  differences someone if you could just  say a little bit more about gender  gender is complicated because it's  deeply inflected by class  I think the stereotype is that the  competent woman are the terminal' class  woman Nancy Fraser has a wonderful  meant about progressive neoliberalism  and the lien in argument or the  progressive you know version of  neoliberalism which is that as long as  the glass ceiling is broken by  upper-middle class woman were fine but  in fact this leads the majority of women  trailing so this very much calls for a  much deeper conversation about the  criteria by which the worth of people is  acknowledged so that's all I will say  Michelle thank you very much for a truly  wonderful talk well now I'll call a barb  Sami that's our board chair to say the  last word barb well thank you everyone  for the wonderful evening and let me add  my thanks to Alan's Michaele 